.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
int \fBgf_key_generate\fP (gf_key **out, enum gf_keytype type, unsigned bits);
Generate a key.  BITS is used for RSA only (at least GF_MIN_RSA_BITS, default 3072 if 0).
.TP
int \fBgf_key_from_blob\fP (gf_key **out, const uint8_t *blob, size_t len);
Parse a public key blob.  The result has no private part.
.TP
int \fBgf_key_from_openssh_private\fP (gf_key **out, const char *text, size_t len);
Read an unencrypted OpenSSH private key file ("\-\-\-\-\-BEGIN OPENSSH PRIVATE KEY\-\-\-\-\-", the format of ssh\-keygen \-N ''), given as text. A passphrase\-protected key is refused with GF_ERR_UNSUPPORTED; see gf_key_from_openssh_private_encrypted.
.TP
int \fBgf_key_from_text\fP (gf_key **out, const char *text, size_t len, gf_slice *comment);
The one\-line text form of a public key, as in ssh\-keygen's .pub files and (without options) authorized_keys: "TYPE BASE64 [COMMENT]".  Blanks before and a line end after are allowed, and nothing after that.  TYPE must be the name inside the key; the base64 must be exact (padded, no stray bits). Refused as gf_key_from_blob refuses.  If COMMENT is not NULL it is set to the comment, which points into TEXT (empty if there is none).
.TP
int \fBgf_key_to_text\fP (const gf_key *key, char *out, size_t cap, size_t *outlen);
Write "TYPE BASE64" and a NUL, without a comment or a line end.  OUTLEN, if not NULL, receives the length without the NUL.
.SS "Passphrase\-protected private key files"
.PP
OpenSSH encrypts a private key file with a key derived from the passphrase by bcrypt\-pbkdf (OpenBSD's, from the bcrypt password hash), which libgcrypt does not have and which this library does not implement: the application supplies it.  KDF is called with the passphrase, the file's salt and round count (1 to GF_KDF_MAX_ROUNDS), and must write OUTLEN bytes (the cipher's key and then its IV, at most 48) to OUT, as bcrypt_pbkdf(passphrase, salt, rounds, out, outlen) of OpenBSD's libc, OpenSSH's openbsd\-compat and libbcrypt do.  Return 0 for success.  The passphrase is not copied and not kept; the output is wiped after use.
.PP
The file ciphers are aes128\-ctr, aes192\-ctr, aes256\-ctr, which ssh\-keygen uses by default, and aes128\-gcm@openssh.com and aes256\-gcm@openssh.com, which it can; the other ciphers it knows (CBC, 3DES, ChaCha20) are refused.  Counter mode does not authenticate the file: a wrong passphrase is told by two check values in the plaintext, and a file damaged on the disk by nothing (it is the owner's to protect, not the network's).  GCM does, and then a wrong passphrase or a damaged file fails its tag.  Both give GF_ERR_PASSPHRASE.
.TP
int \fBgf_key_from_openssh_private_encrypted\fP (gf_key **out, const char *text, size_t len, const char *passphrase, gf_bcrypt_pbkdf_fn kdf, void *user);
As gf_key_from_openssh_private, for a file that may be encrypted.  For an unencrypted file PASSPHRASE and KDF are not used.  An encrypted file with no passphrase or no KDF is refused with GF_ERR_UNSUPPORTED, a wrong passphrase gives GF_ERR_PASSPHRASE, and a KDF that fails GF_ERR_HANDLER.
.TP
int \fBgf_key_to_openssh_private_encrypted\fP (const gf_key *key, char *out, size_t cap, size_t *outlen, const char *passphrase, uint32_t rounds, gf_bcrypt_pbkdf_fn kdf, void *user);
Write the key encrypted with PASSPHRASE: aes256\-gcm@openssh.com, a random 16\-byte salt, ROUNDS (0: 16, as ssh\-keygen \-a) of bcrypt\-pbkdf by KDF.  OpenSSH 7.x and later read the result.  Otherwise as gf_key_to_openssh_private.
.TP
int \fBgf_key_to_openssh_private\fP (const gf_key *key, char *out, size_t cap, size_t *outlen);
Write the private key in the same format, as PEM text.
.TP
