Sign DATA with ALG.  The signature blob (RFC 4253 section 6.6: string algorithm name, string signature) is written to OUT.  The key must have a private part and ALG must belong to its type.
.TP
int \fBgf_key_verify\fP (const gf_key *key, enum gf_sigalg alg, const uint8_t *data, size_t len, const uint8_t *sig, size_t siglen);
Verify a signature blob on DATA.  The blob must carry the name of ALG. Returns GF_OK, or GF_ERR_HOSTKEY for a signature that does not verify or is malformed.
.SS "FIDO security keys (PROTOCOL.u2f)"
.PP
A security key signs on a token held by its user, and the library never talks to one: these keys exist here to be verified, by a server that authenticates users.  "sk\-ssh\-ed25519@openssh.com" and "sk\-ecdsa\-sha2\-nistp256@openssh.com" keys are an Ed25519 or P\-256 key with an application string, which must begin "ssh:".  Their signatures carry a flags byte and a counter after the signature itself, and sign SHA256(application) || flags || counter || SHA256(message), so that a signature can not be taken from one relying party to another.  They can not be generated, read from a private key file or used to sign here, and are never host keys.  A signature made with the token's "user presence" flag clear is not accepted.
.TP
int \fBgf_key_is_security_key\fP (const gf_key *key);
.TP
const uint8_t *\fBgf_key_application\fP (const gf_key *key, size_t *len);
The application of a security key (not NUL\-terminated), or NULL.
.TP
int \fBgf_key_verify_sk\fP (const gf_key *key, enum gf_sigalg alg, const uint8_t *data, size_t len, const uint8_t *sig, size_t siglen, uint8_t *flags, uint32_t *counter);
Verify a security key's signature as gf_key_verify does and report its flags and counter (either may be NULL).  The user presence flag must be set, or the signature is refused.  The counter is not checked: it only means something to a server that remembers the last one.
.SS "OpenSSH certificates (PROTOCOL.certkeys)"
.PP
A certificate is a public key signed by a certificate authority (a CA key) with the names it may be used for and the times it is good for. gf_key_from_blob accepts the certificate blob types for Ed25519, ECDSA and RSA keys and returns the certificate as a key: its blob is the certificate, its type name the certificate's ("ssh\-ed25519\-cert\-v01@ openssh.com"), and it signs and verifies with the algorithms of policy.h that name certificates.  It is refused if its structure is malformed (options not in order, a bad type), if the key inside it or the CA key is one the policy refuses, or if the CA's signature over it does not verify.  A CA that signed with SHA\-1 ("ssh\-rsa") is refused.
.PP
That the signature verifies shows only that the certificate is what the CA made.  Whether to trust the CA, the principals, the times and the options is for gf_cert_check and the caller: nothing here trusts anything.
.TP
int \fBgf_key_is_certificate\fP (const gf_key *key);
.br
int \fBgf_key_certificate_info\fP (const gf_key *cert, gf_cert_info *info);
.TP
const uint8_t *\fBgf_key_plain_blob\fP (const gf_key *key, size_t *len);
The plain key inside a certificate (same key without the wrapper), as a blob.  For a plain key, its own blob.
.TP
int \fBgf_cert_principal_next\fP (const gf_key *cert, size_t *pos, gf_slice *name);
.br
int \fBgf_cert_critical_next\fP (const gf_key *cert, size_t *pos, gf_slice *name, gf_slice *data);
.br
int \fBgf_cert_extension_next\fP (const gf_key *cert, size_t *pos, gf_slice *name, gf_slice *data);
Iterate the principals, the critical options and the extensions of a certificate: start with *POS = 0; each call returns 1 and advances, or 0 at the end.
.TP
int \fBgf_cert_has_extension\fP (const gf_key *cert, const char *name);
.TP
int \fBgf_cert_has_principal\fP (const gf_key *cert, const char *name, size_t n, int wildcards);
1 if NAME is one of the principals.  With WILDCARDS, a principal may contain '*' and '?', as host certificates do.  A certificate with no principals names nobody here.
.TP
int \fBgf_cert_check\fP (const gf_key *cert, int type, uint64_t now, const char **why);
Is the certificate usable now, as a certificate of TYPE?  NOW is wall time in seconds since 1970.  GF_OK, or GF_ERR_HOSTKEY with *WHY (if not NULL) a short reason: wrong type, not yet valid, expired, a critical option on a host certificate, a critical option this library does not know.  The known critical options of a user certificate are "force\-command", "source\-address" and "verify\-required"; honouring them is the caller's (see auth.h), and a "force\-command" or "source\-address" that is empty or not a single string is refused here.
.TP
int \fBgf_cert_critical_option\fP (const gf_key *cert, const char *name, gf_slice *value);
The value of the critical option NAME of a user certificate: the string inside its data ("force\-command" is the command, "source\-address" a comma\-separated list of addresses and CIDR ranges).  Returns 1 with *VALUE (valid while the certificate is) if present, 0 if absent, and \-1 if present but malformed.
.TP
int \fBgf_cert_source_address_ok\fP (const gf_key *cert, const char *address);
Does the certificate's "source\-address" option, if it has one, allow ADDRESS (the client's address in text, IPv4 or IPv6; an IPv4\-mapped IPv6 address counts as the IPv4 one)?  1 if there is no such option. If there is one and ADDRESS is NULL or not an address, or the option is malformed, 0: an address restriction that cannot be checked is not met.
.SS "Making certificates"
.PP
gf_cert_sign is the ssh\-keygen \-s of this library: it makes a certificate for SUBJECT, signed by the CA key, and returns it as a key exactly as gf_key_from_blob would (it is made by that, so the certificate returned is one this library accepts).  gf_key_to_text gives the line for a ".pub" file, and gf_key_blob the bytes.
.PP
.nf
.RS
It refuses what would be a mistake to sign:
  \- a certificate with no principals (nobody here: gf_cert_has_principal),
    or whose key ID or names have control characters;
  \- one with no end to its validity, or that ends before it begins: the
    times are the caller's, in seconds since 1970, and every certificate
    must end;
  \- a serial number of 0, which no revocation list can name (krl.h);
  \- a user certificate with a source address that is not a list of
    addresses and ranges, or a host certificate with any user option.
Permissions are an allow list: with none of the GF_CERT_ALLOW_ bits a
user certificate permits nothing (ssh\-keygen \-O clear), where ssh\-keygen
by default permits all five.
.RE
.fi
.PP
The CA key must have a private part and be a plain key; it signs with the first algorithm of gf_key_sigalgs (never ssh\-rsa), or ALG if that is not 0.  SUBJECT may be a public key or a key pair, and not a certificate.
.TP
int \fBgf_cert_sign\fP (gf_key **out, const gf_key *ca, const gf_key *subject, const gf_cert_spec *spec, enum gf_sigalg alg);
.TP
int \fBgf_key_with_certificate\fP (const gf_key *key, const gf_key *cert, gf_key **out);
Attach CERT to KEY, which must have a private part and be the certificate's own key.  The result is a key that presents the certificate and signs for it; KEY is unchanged.
